physics.c:  float gravity = 9.81f;
   bytes in memory:  c3 f5 1c 41

report.c:   extern int gravity;   printf("%d", gravity);
   same bytes, read as int:  1092416963

Neither file is wrong on its own, and neither tool objected.

What the linker compares:
   physics.o defines:  name=gravity  size=4  bind=GLOBAL  kind=OBJECT
   report.o  uses:     name=gravity  size=4  bind=GLOBAL  kind=OBJECT
   names match, sizes match  ->  linked, no diagnostic

There is no field in that entry for 'float' or 'int'. There is nowhere
to put one. The compiler knew, and threw it away.

Contrast, if physics.c had used a double instead:
   double is 8 bytes, int is 4 bytes
   low 4 bytes read as int:  1374389535
   a size-comparing linker could at least warn here -- 8 != 4.
   float vs int gives it nothing to notice: 4 == 4.
